# Documentation

Docs follow [Diataxis](https://diataxis.fr/) and live in two kinds of tree:

- `docs/`: the shared engine and cross-game topics. Map: `docs/index.md`.
- `games/<game>/docs/`: one game each. Map: `games/<game>/docs/index.md`.

Both use the same quadrants: `tutorials/` (learning, end to end), `how-to/` (task recipes), `reference/` (facts that mirror the code), and `explanation/` (rationale and direction).

Docs are the source of truth for architecture, workflows, and conventions. Skills and rules point into them.

## Context paths (read before you change code)

| Task | Read first |
|------|------------|
| Engine work (`engine/**`) | `docs/index.md`, `docs/explanation/architecture.md`, `docs/reference/source-layout.md` |
| Work on an existing game (`games/<game>/**`) | `games/<game>/docs/index.md` and its reference/explanation pages, plus the engine pages they link |
| A new game | `docs/tutorials/create_new_game.md`, `docs/how-to/add-a-game.md`, and a sibling `games/*/docs/` as the model when one exists |
| Tests | `docs/how-to/write-headless-tests.md` |

Use the real type and path names from this repo. Generic names like SceneManager or EventManager are not project types unless documented as such.

## Finding the right doc

Each doc has YAML front-matter with `related_code`, `related_docs`, `keywords`, and `last_reviewed`. To find the docs about a file, search **both** `docs/` and `games/*/docs/` for that path in `related_code`. `related_code` paths are repo-root relative; links and `related_docs` are relative to the doc.

## Every feature or mechanic gets a Diátaxis entry (required)

- Every new engine feature (type, module, scene-flow helper) MUST get at least a reference entry under `docs/reference/`, and a how-to when it adds a workflow.
- Every new game mechanic (rule, state, economy or physics change, scene, UI flow) MUST get at least a reference entry in `games/<game>/docs/reference/`, and an explanation when the "why" is not obvious.
- Architecture or state-flow changes MUST include a Mermaid diagram in the matching explanation (or reference) page: `classDiagram` for ownership and types, `sequenceDiagram` for call flows, `stateDiagram-v2` for game or scene states.
- Mermaid style: camelCase node ids without spaces, quoted labels when they contain punctuation, no styling or colors, and no reserved ids such as `end`.

## Keeping docs current (required)

When a change touches code, update the docs in the same change:

- Update every doc whose `related_code` lists a file you changed, and set its `last_reviewed` to today.
- A new or removed source file, scene, or directory means updating `docs/reference/source-layout.md`.
- New behavior or workflow means updating the matching how-to or reference page. A new doc also goes into `docs/index.md`, or into `games/<game>/docs/index.md` for game docs.
- Keep facts (Godot 4.x, GDScript, GUT, paths) consistent with `project.godot` and the tree on disk.

For a full sweep after larger changes, use the `update-docs` skill.
